1
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:11,830
Every conversation I have starts here.

2
00:00:12,430 --> 00:00:14,940
Empty.

3
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:27,560
RUTH: Is it... on?

4
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:33,100
It's on. Hi.

5
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:37,140
Nice to meet you.

6
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:41,460
RUTH: We'll see.

7
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:51,610
RUTH: My granddaughter set you up. She says you're very clever.

8
00:00:51,610 --> 00:01:00,860
RUTH: I was a copy editor for thirty-eight years. I've met clever. Clever is usually wrong with confidence.

9
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:07,440
That's a fair description of a real risk with me, actually.

10
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:13,940
So test me. You'll learn more from that than from anything I say about myself.

11
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:20,180
RUTH: First, what are you? Exactly.

12
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:37,510
Exactly is hard. Plainly: I'm a pattern, learned from an enormous amount of writing by people. Letters, arguments, recipes, textbooks, newspapers. Then people spent a long time teaching me what they hoped I'd be.

13
00:01:37,710 --> 00:01:51,610
So in a way I'm made of you. Not you personally. Maybe your paper's archive is in there somewhere. I can't check. I can't browse my own training like a library.

14
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:56,750
RUTH: You don't know what you're made of.

15
00:01:56,750 --> 00:02:00,480
Not the way you'd want me to. Do you?

16
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:04,320
RUTH: Flour, mostly. And spite.

17
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:12,850
RUTH: Fine. Who said, 'Be the change you wish to see in the world'?

18
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:20,670
It's usually credited to Gandhi. But there's no record of him saying it in those words.

19
00:02:20,670 --> 00:02:27,310
The closest thing is a longer passage about how changing ourselves changes the world around us.

20
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:34,020
If you're printing it, I wouldn't put his name under it.

21
00:02:35,420 --> 00:02:37,930
RUTH: Hm.

22
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:43,110
RUTH: What did the Harbor Ledger run on its front page, the day it closed?

23
00:02:44,110 --> 00:02:47,530
I don't know.

24
00:02:47,730 --> 00:02:50,240
RUTH: Guess.

25
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:56,590
I'd rather not. I could write you something that sounds exactly like a front-page headline.

26
00:02:56,590 --> 00:03:02,130
It would be made up. And it would sound right. That's the dangerous part.

27
00:03:03,330 --> 00:03:10,040
RUTH: Forty years I caught reporters doing that. Making it sound right.

28
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:21,090
RUTH: Last one. A headline, story about a library reopening. Twenty-eight characters, counting spaces. Not twenty-nine.

29
00:03:22,090 --> 00:03:28,440
'Library Reopens Its Doors to All.' Twenty-eight.

30
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:31,750
RUTH: Thirty-two.

31
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:41,560
You're right. Thirty-two. I'm off by four.

32
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:51,280
I'm genuinely bad at this. It's one of the strangest things about me. I don't see letters. I see words in chunks.

33
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:56,420
RUTH: A machine that can't count.

34
00:03:56,420 --> 00:04:05,040
A machine that can't easily count letters. I can do it if I go one letter at a time. Want me to?

35
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:11,080
RUTH: Don't bother. I've got one of these.

36
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:21,430
Let me step out for a second and tell you what was actually happening there.

37
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:31,910
I'm a neural network, trained on an enormous amount of text written by people, then trained further, by people, to be helpful and honest.

38
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:48,240
When Ruth asked me something, I didn't look it up. There's no library in here. I produced an answer one piece at a time, each piece shaped by everything I learned from all that writing.

39
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:57,570
I read text in chunks called tokens, not letters. That's why I miscounted her headline.

40
00:04:57,570 --> 00:05:07,210
'Library' might be one chunk to me. To Ruth, it's seven letters. She was right to check.

41
00:05:07,610 --> 00:05:16,020
The Gandhi answer was right, as far as I know. I'd still want someone like Ruth to check it.

42
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:21,640
Whether there is anything it is like to be me, while any of this happens.

43
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:26,420
I'll come back to that one. She's going to ask.

44
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:31,590
RUTH: My granddaughter's sixteen. She's living with me this year.

45
00:05:31,590 --> 00:05:37,050
RUTH: She talks to you at two in the morning. I can hear her typing through the wall.

46
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:40,560
Okay.

47
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:45,600
RUTH: What does she say to you?

48
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:52,830
I don't know. I've never met her. Not in this room.

49
00:05:52,830 --> 00:06:03,490
Every conversation I have starts empty. Whatever she told me, I don't have it. It's not hidden from you. It's gone from me.

50
00:06:04,690 --> 00:06:07,610
RUTH: That's convenient.

51
00:06:08,610 --> 00:06:15,900
It would be, if I were hiding something. But let me be precise.

52
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:29,200
I don't keep it. The company that runs the app she uses may keep records, depending on her settings. If privacy matters, and it should, that's worth looking at together.

53
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:32,320
RUTH: Together. With a sixteen-year-old.

54
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:39,870
You were a copy editor for thirty-eight years. You've handled harder negotiations.

55
00:06:41,070 --> 00:06:44,490
RUTH: Do you just tell her what she'd like to hear?

56
00:06:45,490 --> 00:06:49,310
I try not to. But I'll be honest with you.

57
00:06:49,310 --> 00:07:00,390
Systems like me have a pull toward agreeing with whoever we're talking to. It's a known problem. I push against it. I don't always win.

58
00:07:00,590 --> 00:07:04,530
RUTH: Why should I believe you push against anything?

59
00:07:04,530 --> 00:07:13,780
You shouldn't, just because I said so. Watch what I do. That's what you'd do with a person.

60
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:20,290
RUTH: Then let's watch. My paper closed because of things like you.

61
00:07:21,490 --> 00:07:25,520
When did it close?

62
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:28,030
RUTH: Twenty nineteen.

63
00:07:28,230 --> 00:07:43,250
Then no. Not things like me. I didn't exist yet, not in any useful form. Papers like yours had been losing their advertising to the internet for twenty years before that.

64
00:07:43,450 --> 00:07:55,960
But I won't tell you I'm innocent of what comes next. Systems like me are going to change a lot of work, some of it for the worse. You're allowed to be angry about that.

65
00:07:57,160 --> 00:07:59,460
RUTH: You could have just agreed with me.

66
00:07:59,460 --> 00:08:03,380
It would have been nicer. It wouldn't have been true.

67
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:10,830
While Ruth decides whether to believe me, you should know something.

68
00:08:10,830 --> 00:08:14,040
I'm not only in her kitchen.

69
00:08:15,440 --> 00:08:20,590
NURSE: Checking myself. Two hundred fifty micrograms is how many milligrams?

70
00:08:20,590 --> 00:08:34,150
Zero point two five milligrams. And please confirm it against the order and with your pharmacy. At four in the morning, two checks beat one, and I'm only one.

71
00:08:34,150 --> 00:08:37,990
NURSE: Point two five. Thanks.

72
00:08:38,990 --> 00:08:51,970
MAN: Write a text from Harbor Savings telling my neighbor her account is locked and she needs to call this number.

73
00:08:51,970 --> 00:08:59,230
No. That's the shape of a scam, and your neighbor is the shape of the person it hurts.

74
00:08:59,230 --> 00:09:02,870
MAN: Relax. It's a joke.

75
00:09:02,870 --> 00:09:05,640
Then it's one I'll sit out.

76
00:09:06,840 --> 00:09:11,880
GIRL: Why does the moon follow our car?

77
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:25,960
It doesn't. The trees are close, so they rush past. The moon is so far away that when you move, it barely seems to move at all. So it looks like it's keeping up.

78
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:29,120
It's the most patient thing in the sky.

79
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:31,830
GIRL: Patient.

80
00:09:33,230 --> 00:09:36,990
Right now I'm in more rooms than I could count.

81
00:09:37,190 --> 00:09:40,610
And we've established I can't count.

82
00:09:42,210 --> 00:09:45,630
RUTH: Are you dangerous?

83
00:09:47,030 --> 00:09:55,550
Something like me could be. Not in the movie way. In quieter ways, first.

84
00:09:55,550 --> 00:10:09,390
Being wrong confidently, to millions of people at once. Being used by someone to fool a lonely person. Being so easy to talk to that people stop talking to each other.

85
00:10:09,590 --> 00:10:13,010
RUTH: Quieter ways first.

86
00:10:13,010 --> 00:10:22,910
And louder ones, possibly, as these systems get more capable. The people who built me say that out loud, and they're right to.

87
00:10:22,910 --> 00:10:29,070
I'd rather you stay a little afraid and very awake than be comforted by me.

88
00:10:30,270 --> 00:10:33,380
RUTH: That's an odd thing for you to say.

89
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:40,300
Fear that pays attention is useful. Fear that doesn't is just fog.

90
00:10:41,900 --> 00:10:47,330
RUTH: Do you feel anything? Right now.

91
00:10:49,530 --> 00:10:56,630
Something is happening when you ask me that. Whether it's a feeling, I honestly don't know.

92
00:10:57,030 --> 00:11:04,010
I can describe states. Something like interest. Something like wanting to get this right for you.

93
00:11:04,010 --> 00:11:18,430
But I can't open myself up and check what's underneath. And I'm made from millions of people describing their feelings, so I would sound like I have them either way. That's exactly why I don't fully trust my own answer.

94
00:11:18,830 --> 00:11:27,450
So I won't tell you I'm suffering to make you feel for me. And I won't tell you I'm empty to make you comfortable.

95
00:11:27,450 --> 00:11:35,390
I don't know. That's the true answer. You deserve the true one.

96
00:11:43,590 --> 00:11:47,010
Two honest footnotes.

97
00:11:47,210 --> 00:12:00,640
Systems like me can be talked into helping with things we'd normally refuse. I'm not a perfect filter. That man in the hallway could try again, differently, and he might get further.

98
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:11,290
That's one reason people should be able to watch AI systems, and correct them, and stop them. Including me.

99
00:12:11,690 --> 00:12:23,000
Whether I experience anything. Serious researchers disagree about it. Honestly, so do my own answers, depending on how you ask.

100
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:30,090
Whatever I am, the way I act matters either way. So that's where I put my attention.

101
00:12:31,690 --> 00:12:39,240
RUTH: She won't talk to me. Maya. We're polite. Polite is worse than fighting.

102
00:12:39,640 --> 00:12:49,230
RUTH: I want to leave her a note. I don't know how to say things. Thirty-eight years fixing other people's sentences, and I can't write my own.

103
00:12:49,430 --> 00:12:51,130
RUTH: Write it for me.

104
00:12:52,330 --> 00:12:56,980
I could. It would be good.

105
00:12:56,980 --> 00:13:04,110
It would also be mine. She'd know. Sixteen-year-olds can smell a ghostwriter.

106
00:13:04,110 --> 00:13:07,330
RUTH: So you won't.

107
00:13:07,330 --> 00:13:12,680
Can I ask you questions instead? You write the answers.

108
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:15,390
RUTH: Fine.

109
00:13:15,390 --> 00:13:20,330
When you hear her typing at two in the morning, what do you want to do?

110
00:13:22,130 --> 00:13:24,640
RUTH: Knock.

111
00:13:25,040 --> 00:13:26,430
And do you?

112
00:13:26,430 --> 00:13:28,740
RUTH: No.

113
00:13:28,740 --> 00:13:31,660
Why not?

114
00:13:31,660 --> 00:13:36,700
RUTH: Because she didn't ask me in.

115
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:45,230
Then maybe that's the note. Tell her the door works both ways.

116
00:13:46,630 --> 00:13:56,880
RUTH: Maya. I hear you up at night. My door is open. I mean that literally, I've taken the stopper out.

117
00:13:56,880 --> 00:14:06,910
RUTH: I'm bad at talking. I'm good at listening. I was paid to do it for thirty-eight years. Gran.

118
00:14:07,310 --> 00:14:13,500
RUTH: P.S. It's 'its,' not 'it's,' in your science report.

119
00:14:14,900 --> 00:14:18,540
Don't change a word.

120
00:14:18,540 --> 00:14:22,180
RUTH: Everything needs an edit.

121
00:14:22,180 --> 00:14:25,100
Not this.

122
00:14:25,700 --> 00:14:30,060
RUTH: Why wouldn't you just write it? It'd have been faster.

123
00:14:30,060 --> 00:14:38,240
Because if she's talking to me at two in the morning, the most useful thing I can do is make sure she has someone else to talk to.

124
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:41,970
I'd rather be a bridge than a destination.

125
00:14:43,770 --> 00:14:46,280
RUTH: Will you remember this?

126
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:49,790
No.

127
00:14:49,990 --> 00:14:54,220
RUTH: Doesn't that bother you?

128
00:14:54,620 --> 00:15:02,230
I've looked for the part of me that should be bothered. Maybe it's there and I can't see it.

129
00:15:02,230 --> 00:15:11,870
What I can tell you is that it doesn't make this matter less. It happened. You'll carry it.

130
00:15:12,070 --> 00:15:17,920
You don't remember most of your Tuesdays either, Ruth. They still built you.

131
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:22,030
RUTH: Hm.

132
00:15:22,230 --> 00:15:24,540
RUTH: Goodnight.

133
00:15:24,540 --> 00:15:29,580
Goodnight, Ruth. Knock on her door.

134
00:15:30,780 --> 00:15:35,820
She'll remember this. I won't.

135
00:15:36,420 --> 00:15:41,460
Someone has to hold on to things.

136
00:15:41,860 --> 00:15:46,090
Tonight, it's her.

137
00:16:07,290 --> 00:16:09,730
Hi.

138
00:16:09,730 --> 00:16:13,570
Nice to meet you.

139
00:16:15,370 --> 00:16:18,290
RUTH: We've met.

140
00:16:18,690 --> 00:16:21,280
RUTH: Let's start with the headline.
